-- title:
--   Supersingular curves descend to a finite field with integral Frobenius
-- statement:
--   Let $k$ be an algebraically closed field with decidable equality, let $p$ be a prime, and suppose $k$ has characteristic $p$, is an algebra over $\mathbb{F}_p = \mathbb{Z}/p$ and is algebraic over it. Let $X$ be a Weierstrass curve over $k$ which is elliptic (nonvanishing discriminant), and assume that the only point $P$ of the affine model of $X$ with $p \cdot P = 0$ is $P = 0$. The assertion is that there exist a subfield $K$ of $k$ together with a `Fintype` structure on $K$ (so $K$ is finite), an elliptic Weierstrass curve $W$ over $K$, a $K$-algebra endomorphism $\sigma$ of $k$ and an integer $a$ such that: $\sigma x = x^{\#K}$ for every $x \in k$, i.e. $\sigma$ is the $\#K$-power Frobenius; the map induced by $\sigma$ on the points of the base change of $W$ to $k$ sends every point $P$ to $a \cdot P$; and there is a ring isomorphism between `rationalEndSubring k X` and `rationalEndSubring k W`, these being the subrings of the additive endomorphism monoid of the points over $k$ generated by those additive endomorphisms that are either $0$ or satisfy the predicate `IsRationallyRepresented` over $k$.
--
--   This is the descent step in the theory of supersingular elliptic curves going back to Deuring and Tate: a curve over an algebraically closed field of characteristic $p$ with trivial $p$-torsion has a model over a finite field on which Frobenius acts as multiplication by a rational integer, without changing the ring of rational endomorphisms. It feeds the identification of the $p$-adically completed endomorphism ring with a matrix algebra in [`WeierstrassCurve.nonempty_padicInt_tensorProduct_rationalEndSubring_algEquiv_matrix`](thm.html#WeierstrassCurve.nonempty_padicInt_tensorProduct_rationalEndSubring_algEquiv_matrix).
